**Concept of Self-Referential Structures:**
A self-referential structure in C is a user-defined struct that contains one or more member fields which are pointers to a structure of the identical type. They are declared as:

```c
struct Node {
    int data;
    struct Node *next; // Pointer to same structure type
};

```

**Usage in Dynamic Data Structures:**
Because dynamic structures such as linked lists or trees grow dynamically at runtime, they cannot rely on contiguous stack memory. By employing a self-referential pointer (`next`), individual structures (nodes) allocated dynamically in heap space can store pointers to their logical successors, effectively establishing chained relationships.

**Illustrative Example & Code Snippet:**

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

// Definition of self-referential structure
struct Node {
    int data;
    struct Node *next;
};

int main() {
    // Allocate nodes dynamically in memory heap
    struct Node* first = (struct Node*)malloc(sizeof(struct Node));
    struct Node* second = (struct Node*)malloc(sizeof(struct Node));

    // Populate values and link elements
    first->data = 10;
    first->next = second; // Self-referential address assignment

    second->data = 20;
    second->next = NULL;  // Termination pointer

    // Traversal demonstration
    struct Node* temp = first;
    while(temp != NULL) {
        printf("%d -> ", temp->data);
        temp = temp->next;
    }
    printf("NULL\n");

    // Free memory blocks
    free(first);
    free(second);
    return 0;
}
```

This scenario can be modeled as a **Singly Linked List** where each node represents a train compartment, and the list `head` points to the engine node.

**Data Structure Definition:**

```c
struct Compartment {
    int compartment_id;
    struct Compartment* next;
};

```

**1. Attaching a compartment in between compartments:**
To link a new compartment with ID `new_id` immediately behind the compartment referenced by `prev_compartment`:

```c
void attach_compartment(struct Compartment* prev_compartment, int new_id) {
    if (prev_compartment == NULL) {
        printf("Error: Previous compartment node is invalid.\n");
        return;
    }
    // 1. Instantiate the new compartment node
    struct Compartment* new_comp = (struct Compartment*)malloc(sizeof(struct Compartment));
    new_comp->compartment_id = new_id;

    // 2. Adjust link pointers
    new_comp->next = prev_compartment->next;
    prev_compartment->next = new_comp;
}

```

**2. Detaching a compartment in between compartments:**
To disconnect and release the compartment node that follows `prev_compartment`:

```c
void detach_compartment(struct Compartment* prev_compartment) {
    if (prev_compartment == NULL || prev_compartment->next == NULL) {
        printf("Error: No successor compartment available to detach.\n");
        return;
    }
    // 1. Reference the targeted node
    struct Compartment* temp = prev_compartment->next;

    // 2. Bypass the targeted node
    prev_compartment->next = temp->next;

    // 3. Deallocate memory of detached node
    free(temp);
}
```
